rules_version = '2';

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Firestore security rules — The Bourbon Cup
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ── Read this before changing anything ────────────────────────────
// Two gates, and only the second one matters.
//
// People sign in with Google or Apple (src/lib/auth.js), which proves who
// they are and nothing else — anybody can make a Google account, and the
// app's Firebase config ships in the bundle by design, so "signed in" on
// its own would let any stranger who viewed source rewrite the tournament.
//
// So writing requires MEMBERSHIP: a bc_accounts/{uid} document, which an
// account can only create by presenting the tournament password. The
// password lives in bc_secrets/access, which only a director may read; the
// rules compare against it with `get()`, which is not subject to read
// rules, so the check works the same for everybody else.
// That is what makes it a lock rather than a doorman — it holds against
// someone reading the bundle, disabling the JavaScript, or skipping the
// app entirely and talking to Firestore with the public config.
//
// Reads stay open, deliberately: the leaderboard is meant to be glanceable
// by a spouse on the couch with a link, and the push service worker has no
// user to sign in as. If that ever needs to change, `isOpen()` is the one
// place to change it.
//
// Membership is not the last word: what Admin owns — the roster, rounds,
// matches, courses, groups, tee sheets, settings, editions and the
// password — requires DIRECTOR on top of it, which is a flag no client can
// set (see isDirector below). Members write the things players actually do
// from a tee box: scores, skins, CTPs, card signatures, the round lock,
// their own push token, and the one narrow update that claims their own
// name off the roster.
//
// With one exception, and it is scoped to a tournament that does not exist:
// inside a DEMO edition (`is_demo` on its bc_editions row, written only by
// `npm run seed:demo`), any member is an administrator. See canAdminEdition
// below for why that is real access rather than a read-only Admin screen, and
// for the three things it still cannot do.
//
// Within each half there is no per-document ownership, deliberately.
// Scoring is communal — one phone posts for a group of four, the director
// fixes anybody's card — so "only your own rows" would break the actual
// workflow before it stopped anybody. The round gate, the finalize lock
// and the card signatures remain the client-side guards on top.
//
// ── Deploying this ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Rules are deployed by hand and the app is not. Deploy these only once
// the matching build is live and everyone's phone has loaded it (a
// pull-to-refresh will do it). Deploy them first and any phone on an
// older bundle — including one signed in but with no membership document
// yet — silently fails every write it attempts: scores typed on the tee
// that never arrive.
//
// ── Locking somebody out ──────────────────────────────────────────
// Delete their bc_accounts document in the Firebase console. Nothing in
// the app can do it (update and delete are denied to every client), which
// is deliberate. Rotating the password afterwards does NOT evict anybody
// already through the door — existing memberships are never re-checked
// against it.
//
// ── Making somebody a director ────────────────────────────────────
// A director does it from the app: Admin → Players → the crown in the
// player modal, which writes `is_director` on that person's bc_accounts
// document. The app reads the same flag to decide whether to show the
// Admin tab, so what is on screen is what these rules will honour.
//
// Nobody can set it on themselves — not at create time, not after — so
// knowing the password never promotes anybody, and a director cannot
// demote themselves either, which means the set can never be emptied from
// inside the app.
//
// THE FIRST ONE HAS TO COME FROM THIS CONSOLE, because the rule requires a
// director to already exist: on their bc_accounts/{uid} document, add
// `is_director` (boolean) = true. That is also the way back if the set is
// ever emptied, and the way somebody steps down.
//
// Consequence worth knowing before publishing: until one membership
// document carries the flag, nobody can edit the tournament at all. Set it
// before these rules land, not after.
//
// The crown drawn in the roster LIST is read from these same membership
// documents. The `isDirector` field on bc_players is vestigial — nothing
// reads it. Rules never could: finding the roster row whose auth_uid is
// yours would need a query, and rules only fetch paths they can construct.
//
// ── What this replaces ────────────────────────────────────────────
// Until this file, the deployed rules were a single blanket grant:
//
//   match /{document=**} { allow read, write: if true; }
//
// So the enumeration below IS the change. The list was taken from the
// collections the client actually touches (`db.subscribe` / `db.get` /
// `db.upsert` / `db.delete` in src/firebase.js, which is the only
// Firestore entry point in the repo — pipeline/ and api/ never reach
// Firestore), so anything newly denied is by construction something no
// code path here reads or writes. Data in such a collection is untouched,
// just unreachable until a line is added.
//
// Deploy with:  firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
//
// There are tests, and this file is the reason to run them — see
// firestore.rules.test.mjs for the one-liner. They cover the password
// path specifically: wrong code refused, code unreadable, non-member
// refused, and nobody minting a membership for somebody else's uid.

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // Reading is open to anyone with the app — a shared leaderboard link,
    // the push service worker, a phone that has not signed in yet.
    function isOpen() {
      return true;
    }

    function isSignedIn() {
      return request.auth != null;
    }

    // ── Membership ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Writing needs more than an account: it needs a membership document,
    // issued only to someone who presented the tournament password (see
    // bc_accounts below). Signing in is free — anybody can make a Google
    // account — so this is the predicate that actually decides who can
    // change the tournament, and it holds against someone talking to
    // Firestore directly rather than through the app.
    //
    // Costs one document read per write. At a dozen phones for a weekend
    // that is nothing.
    function isMember() {
      return isSignedIn()
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_accounts/$(request.auth.uid));
    }

    // ── Directors ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // A flag on the membership document, and one that NO CLIENT CAN SET:
    // `create` below rejects any attempt to include it, and `update` is
    // denied outright, so the only way it gets there is a human editing
    // the document in the Firebase console (the admin SDK bypasses these
    // rules). That is the point — director is the one role that should not
    // be grantable from a phone, by a stray tap or by anybody who works
    // out what the app is talking to.
    //
    // It deliberately does NOT read the crown on the roster
    // (bc_players.isDirector). Rules cannot search for the roster row whose
    // auth_uid is yours — `get()` needs a path it can construct, and the
    // uid lives in a field — so that crown stays a label on screen and
    // this flag is what actually grants anything.
    //
    // `get(key, default)` rather than `.is_director`, so a membership
    // document written before this existed reads as false instead of
    // erroring the whole evaluation.
    function isDirector() {
      return isMember()
        && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_accounts/$(request.auth.uid))
             .data.get('is_director', false) == true;
    }

    // ── A LOCKED EDITION ───────────────────────────────────────────
    // `locked: true` on bc_editions/{id} freezes that tournament against
    // everybody but a director. See src/lib/editionLock.js for what it means
    // and why it is its own field rather than `status == "archived"`; this is
    // what makes it true.
    //
    // Why it has to live HERE and not in the app: a membership is not
    // edition-scoped. `isMember()` asks whether you presented the tournament
    // password, full stop — it does not ask which year you are looking at, and
    // ☰ → Tournaments is offered to every member, not just directors. So any
    // member can switch to any edition and write to it. During a store beta
    // that is a dozen strangers-to-the-data with the live tournament one tap
    // away, and hiding the picker would not help: the app's Firebase config
    // ships in the bundle, so anybody can talk to Firestore directly.
    //
    // Until this existed, the confirm on opening an archived year told people
    // it was read-only and NOTHING enforced that. The rounds were imported
    // LOCKED and FINAL and the UI declined to draw edit controls for one,
    // which stops a fat finger and stops nothing else.
    //
    // Default UNLOCKED, three times over — a missing edition document, a
    // missing `locked` field, and an edition id that is empty all evaluate to
    // writable. That direction is deliberate. Eleven years of edition rows
    // predate this field, and a default of "locked" would freeze every one of
    // them on deploy; worse, `ensureActiveEditionDoc` seeds the row for a new
    // year lazily, so there is a real window in which a phone is pointed at an
    // edition whose index document does not exist yet. Failing open there
    // means a new tournament starts; failing closed means it cannot.
    function editionLocked(tid) {
      return tid != ''
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_editions/$(tid))
        && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_editions/$(tid))
             .data.get('locked', false) == true;
    }

    // BOTH ends of the write are checked, which matters on update: without the
    // `request.resource` half a member could take a document that lives in a
    // locked year and rewrite it — tournament_id included — into an unlocked
    // one, which is an edit to the frozen tournament by another name.
    //
    // `resource` is null on create and `request.resource` is null on delete,
    // so each side is guarded before its data is touched.
    function editionOpen() {
      return !(resource != null && editionLocked(resource.data.get('tournament_id', '')))
          && !(request.resource != null && editionLocked(request.resource.data.get('tournament_id', '')));
    }

    // What every edition-scoped MEMBER write is gated on. Directors are exempt
    // — somebody has to be able to correct a locked year, and a flag that can
    // strand a tournament beyond repair is a worse bug than the one it fixes.
    // Unlocking is also how you get back, and bc_editions is deliberately NOT
    // gated on this: a lock that blocked its own removal is a one-way door.
    //
    // Costs one document read per write, on top of the one isMember() already
    // does. That is the whole bargain and it is worth naming: a tournament's
    // ~1,300 hole scores make ~1,300 extra reads a weekend, and it is the only
    // thing standing between a beta tester and a live tournament's scorecards.
    // `exists` and `get` on the same path inside one evaluation are billed
    // once.
    //
    // Order matters for that cost: editionOpen() is checked first so the
    // common case — an unlocked year — never pays for the isDirector() read.
    // A demo-only membership (see demoAccessCode) is confined to demo
    // editions by the same both-ends check a demo administrator gets, so a
    // reviewer cannot take a document out of the demo or drag one into it.
    // Asked LAST, so an ordinary member never pays for it: the common case
    // short-circuits on the two clauses in front.
    function canWriteEdition() {
      return isMember()
        && (editionOpen() || isDirector())
        && (!demoOnlyMember() || bothEndsDemo());
    }

    // ── ADMIN INSIDE A DEMO ────────────────────────────────────────
    // Is `tid` a demo tournament? The same `is_demo` flag lib/editionLock
    // reads, off the same document editionLocked() already fetches, so within
    // one evaluation it is billed once. Absent means NO: an edition nobody
    // marked is a real one, which matches createEdition — it never writes the
    // flag, so a tournament a director makes is real unless the seed said
    // otherwise.
    function editionIsDemo(tid) {
      return tid != ''
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_editions/$(tid))
        && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_editions/$(tid))
             .data.get('is_demo', false) == true;
    }

    // BOTH ends, for the same reason editionOpen() checks both: without the
    // `request.resource` half a demo administrator could take a demo document
    // and rewrite its tournament_id into the live cup, which is a write to the
    // real tournament by another name. `resource` is null on create and
    // `request.resource` is null on delete, so each side is guarded before its
    // data is touched.
    function bothEndsDemo() {
      return (resource == null || editionIsDemo(resource.data.get('tournament_id', '')))
        && (request.resource == null || editionIsDemo(request.resource.data.get('tournament_id', '')));
    }

    // What the edition-scoped ADMIN collections are gated on: a director
    // anywhere, or any member inside a demo tournament.
    //
    // Why a member gets real access rather than a read-only Admin drawn for
    // them: App Review and a dozen Play testers need to see what the app IS,
    // and the roster, the draw, the courses and the rounds are half of it. The
    // alternative on the table was an Admin that renders and refuses — but
    // AdminView auto-saves on edit and `db.upsert` swallows a rejection, so a
    // decorative Admin would let a reviewer type a name, watch it appear, and
    // find it gone on the next load. An app that lies about a save is a worse
    // thing to hand Apple than an app that lets a tester rename an invented
    // golfer.
    //
    // The blast radius is the demo edition and nothing else. Every document in
    // it was written by `npm run seed:demo`, `--undo --write` rebuilds it, and
    // `bothEndsDemo()` above is what stops anything reaching out of it.
    //
    // Three things stay director-only on purpose, because they are
    // project-wide rather than edition-scoped and no `tournament_id` on a
    // document could scope them: bc_editions (creating and deleting
    // tournaments — including the demo's own row, and including `locked` on
    // the real cup), bc_secrets (the password), and the crown on bc_accounts.
    // The app hides those two cards for a demo administrator rather than
    // showing them and having the rules refuse, for the same no-lying reason.
    //
    // Order matters for cost: isDirector() is asked first, off the membership
    // document isMember() has already read, so a real director writing a real
    // edition never pays for the bc_editions read that bothEndsDemo() does.
    function canAdminEdition() {
      return isDirector() || (isMember() && bothEndsDemo());
    }

    // The password, and the deliberate hole in it. `accessCode()` reads a
    // document no client may read — rules `get()` is not subject to read
    // rules, which is the whole trick: the secret can be compared without
    // ever being served.
    //
    // A missing or blank code means the door is open. That is not an
    // oversight, it is the bootstrap: before anybody has set a password
    // there is no way to present the right one, and without this the very
    // first membership could never be created and the project would be
    // bricked to its own owners.
    function accessCode() {
      return exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_secrets/access)
        ? get(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_secrets/access).data.code
        : null;
    }

    // ── The second code, and why it is not a second password ──────
    // App Review and Play's reviewers need to get INSIDE the app, and the
    // form on both stores wants a credential written down in a box that is
    // then quoted back on every future update review. A tournament password
    // in that box is a password that can never be rotated without breaking
    // the next review, and one that sixteen men say out loud across a table.
    //
    // So there are two codes and they are NOT equals. The tournament's own
    // code mints an ordinary membership. This one mints a membership stamped
    // `demo_only`, and canWriteEdition() refuses that membership everywhere
    // except a demo edition. Somebody who learns the reviewer code — from a
    // store form, from a screenshot, from a leak — gets a sandbox.
    //
    // Absent means there is no second door, not an open one: demoCodeOK()
    // fails closed on a missing or blank value, unlike accessCode(), whose
    // blank IS the bootstrap.
    function demoAccessCode() {
      return exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_secrets/access)
        ? get(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_secrets/access).data.get('demo_code', null)
        : null;
    }

    function demoCodeOK() {
      return demoAccessCode() != null
        && demoAccessCode() is string
        && demoAccessCode() != ''
        && request.resource.data.get('code', null) is string
        && request.resource.data.code.lower() == demoAccessCode().lower();
    }

    // Is the CALLER's membership one of the stamped ones? Read off the same
    // bc_accounts document isMember() and isDirector() already touch, so
    // within one evaluation it is billed once rather than three times.
    function demoOnlyMember() {
      return isSignedIn()
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_accounts/$(request.auth.uid))
        && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_accounts/$(request.auth.uid))
             .data.get('demo_only', false) == true;
    }

    // Compared without case, because this password's life is being said
    // out loud across a table and typed into a phone by somebody who did
    // not see it written down. A capital letter at the front — from the
    // keyboard, or from whoever repeated it — was never a meaningful part
    // of the secret, and it cost an afternoon once.
    //
    // The `is string` guard is load-bearing: `.lower()` on a missing or
    // non-string field raises, and while a raised rule denies the write
    // just the same, denying it deliberately is easier to reason about
    // than denying it by accident. The stored code keeps whatever casing
    // the director typed — only the comparison is flattened, so Admin can
    // still show it back the way it was written.
    function codeOK() {
      return accessCode() == null
        || accessCode() == ''
        || (request.resource.data.get('code', null) is string
            && request.resource.data.code.lower() == accessCode().lower());
    }

    // ── The door ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // One document per account, id = uid, created by the account itself at
    // the password screen. Its existence IS membership; everything below
    // is gated on it.
    //
    // create: your own uid only, and only with the right password, and
    //         never carrying is_director. There is no other way to mint a
    //         membership — not even for a director.
    // read:   your own, plus every membership for a director, who needs to
    //         see who else is one to show it in Admin → Players.
    // update: ONE field, is_director, and only a director may set it, and
    //         never on themselves. See below.
    // delete: nobody. Revoking somebody entirely is deleting their
    //         document in the Firebase console, deliberately — a client
    //         that could delete its own membership could delete somebody
    //         else's if this rule were ever loosened by accident.
    //
    // Why a director may appoint another one, having just said the flag is
    // console-only: because the alternative was worse. Left console-only,
    // the crown in Admin → Players had to be a label that granted nothing,
    // and a director changing it next year would believe they had handed
    // over access when they had not. Two sources of truth, drifting
    // silently, a year apart. Now there is one, the app writes it, and the
    // crown shows what the rules will actually honour.
    //
    // It concedes little. A director can already rewrite the roster, the
    // rounds and the password; appointing someone is not a larger power
    // than that. What stays impossible is the dangerous half: nobody can
    // make THEMSELVES a director, at create time or after, so the flag
    // cannot be self-issued by anyone who merely knows the password.
    //
    // The self exclusion also holds an invariant worth having: a director
    // cannot demote themselves, so whoever is acting stays one, and the
    // set can never be emptied from inside the app. Stepping down entirely
    // is a console edit — the same place the FIRST director has to come
    // from, since this rule requires one to already exist.
    match /bc_accounts/{uid} {
      allow read: if isDirector() || (isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == uid);
      // Two doors. The tournament code mints an ordinary membership and may
      // NOT claim the stamp; the reviewer code mints one that MUST carry it,
      // which is what confines it to the demo. Neither may carry is_director.
      //
      // The client cannot tell which code it typed — bc_secrets is unreadable
      // to it — so lib/accounts asks for an ordinary membership first and
      // retries stamped if that is refused. Two writes for a reviewer, one
      // for everybody else, and no secret leaves the rules.
      allow create: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == uid
                    && !('is_director' in request.resource.data)
                    && ((codeOK() && !('demo_only' in request.resource.data))
                        || (demoCodeOK()
                            && request.resource.data.get('demo_only', false) == true));
      allow update: if isDirector()
                    && request.auth.uid != uid
                    && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                         .hasOnly(['is_director']);
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // The password itself. Read is denied to everyone OUTSIDE the door and
    // that is the load-bearing half: if a stranger could read this document
    // they could read the code and let themselves in, and the gate would be
    // worth nothing. `codeOK()` above compares against it regardless, since
    // rules `get()` is not subject to these read rules.
    //
    // Directors can read it, so Admin can show one a code they have
    // forgotten, and change it. Nobody else can do either.
    match /bc_secrets/{doc} {
      allow read: if isDirector();
      allow write: if isDirector();
    }

    // ── Tournament data ───────────────────────────────────────────
    // Written by the director through the Admin tabs, read by everyone.
    // Every one of these corresponds to a screen only a director can open;
    // the call sites were walked before gating them (src/App.jsx —
    // onAddPlayer, onSetRound, onSetMatch, onAddCourse, onSaveGroups, the
    // Admin save handlers, and lib/editions.js).

    // The roster carries one exception, and it is the reason this block is
    // not a one-liner: claiming a name is an ordinary member writing to a
    // director-owned collection. It is allowed, narrowly — only the
    // auth_* fields may change, only to the claimer's own uid, and only on
    // a row nobody has claimed. So it cannot rename anybody, move a
    // handicap, hand out a crown, or take a name that is already taken.
    // The director's unlink is a plain director write and needs none of it.
    match /bc_players/{doc} {
      allow read: if isOpen();
      allow update: if canWriteEdition()
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasOnly(['auth_uid', 'auth_email', 'auth_provider', 'auth_linked_at'])
        && request.resource.data.auth_uid == request.auth.uid
        && (!('auth_uid' in resource.data)
            || resource.data.auth_uid == null
            || resource.data.auth_uid == request.auth.uid);
      allow write: if canAdminEdition();
    }
    match /bc_courses/{doc}            { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    match /bc_rounds/{doc}             { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    match /bc_matches/{doc}            { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    // Playing groups — who tees off together, one document per round.
    // Group i goes off at slot i of that round's `tee_time` list; see
    // src/lib/groups.js.
    match /bc_groups/{doc}             { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    match /bc_tee_assignments/{doc}    { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    match /bc_hcp_overrides/{doc}      { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    // Frozen per-round handicaps. Written by the auto-lock on the first
    // score of a round as well as by the director; see src/lib/roundLocks.js.
    match /bc_round_locks/{doc}        { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canWriteEdition(); }
    match /bc_settings/{doc}           { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    match /bc_tournament_settings/{doc} { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }
    // ── The archive ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    // An edition document now carries its own `result` — the two team
    // names and the two scores, computed from that year's cards by the
    // app's own engine (src/lib/editionSummary) and written back by a
    // director once the tournament is complete. So the History tab and
    // the Tournaments picker read one list, and the summary of a year
    // cannot disagree with the year itself.
    //
    // That replaces bc_historical, which was a parallel record of the
    // same fact typed by hand — the app used to tell its own director to
    // add years "via Firestore directly for now". Its rules block is gone
    // with it, and nothing is stranded: the collection was never loaded.
    // The ten years of results live in the repo under
    // data/historical sheets and reach Firestore as whole editions, which
    // is what the History tab now summarises.
    //
    // DIRECTOR-ONLY, and deliberately not canAdminEdition(). This collection is
    // the list of tournaments, so a demo administrator writing it could create
    // an edition, delete a real one, or clear `locked` on the live cup — none
    // of which is scoped to the demo even though the demo is where they are
    // standing. It is also the document editionIsDemo() reads, so a write here
    // could mark a real edition as a demo and unlock everything else in one go.
    match /bc_editions/{doc}           { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if isDirector(); }

    // ── Live scoring ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Written by players from the Scoring tab, so these can never be
    // director-only even once sign-in exists.
    match /bc_hole_scores/{doc}        { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canWriteEdition(); }
    match /bc_ctp/{doc}                { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canWriteEdition(); }
    // Card signatures and attestations — one document per signed card. See
    // src/lib/cardSigs. Written by players from the Scoring tab (sign,
    // attest, unsign) and by the director's force-attest, so it belongs in
    // this half of the list rather than with the director-owned data.
    match /bc_card_sigs/{doc}          { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canWriteEdition(); }

    // ── The photo library ─────────────────────────────────────────
    // One document per photo. Not the photo — the INDEX of it: where the
    // bytes are (`host`) and the two URLs to draw them from. See
    // src/lib/media.js for why Firestore holds only the index.
    //
    // Posting a photo is a MEMBER write, like scoring is. The cup is sixteen
    // people at a golf course and the photos are theirs; routing every one
    // through a director would mean nobody posts any.
    //
    // Three things this block does that no other collection here needs:
    //
    //   1. `create` pins uploadedBy to the caller's own uid. Without it a
    //      member could post under somebody else's name — that field is what
    //      the delete rule below trusts, so it has to be unforgeable at the
    //      moment it is written.
    //
    //   2. `update` is the poster's own, and cannot reassign the photo. A
    //      caption fix should not be able to become a change of author, which
    //      is exactly what an unguarded update would allow.
    //
    //   3. `delete` splits on host. A photo still in the bucket is the
    //      poster's to remove; anything else — a year graduated out to the
    //      public library — is director-only, because its bytes are no longer
    //      somewhere the app can delete from and removing the document would
    //      strand a file. lib/media.js canDelete() mirrors this exactly, and
    //      media.test.js pins it.
    //
    // The bucket itself is governed by storage.rules, which CANNOT reproduce
    // the membership gate — Storage rules have no way to read Firestore. That
    // asymmetry is documented in full over there; the short version is that
    // this document is what makes a photo exist in the app, so bytes written
    // without one are invisible.
    //
    // The circuit breaker, enforced rather than suggested. The app hides the
    // upload button when this is tripped (src/lib/media.js), but a hidden
    // button is not a cap — a phone that was mid-upload, or anybody talking to
    // Firestore directly, would sail past it.
    //
    // Costs one or two document reads per photo CREATE. Creates are a few
    // hundred per tournament, so that is nothing; it would be the wrong trade
    // on bc_hole_scores and is not applied there.
    function photoUploadsOn() {
      return !exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_config/photos)
        || get(/databases/$(database)/documents/bc_config/photos)
             .data.get('uploadsDisabled', false) == false;
    }

    match /bc_media/{doc} {
      allow read: if isOpen();
      allow create: if canWriteEdition()
                    && photoUploadsOn()
                    && request.resource.data.uploadedBy == request.auth.uid;
      allow update: if isDirector()
                    || (canWriteEdition()
                        && resource.data.uploadedBy == request.auth.uid
                        && request.resource.data.uploadedBy == resource.data.uploadedBy);
      allow delete: if isDirector()
                    || (canWriteEdition()
                        && resource.data.uploadedBy == request.auth.uid
                        && resource.data.host == 'storage');
    }

    // ── Reporting a photo ─────────────────────────────────────────
    // Both stores ask whether user-generated content can be REPORTED, and
    // until this collection existed the answer was an argument in the review
    // notes. See src/lib/mediaReports.js.
    //
    // READ IS DIRECTOR-ONLY, and that is the unusual line in this file —
    // everything else here is `isOpen()`. In a group of sixteen who have known
    // each other since 2015, "who reported whose photo" is not a fact the
    // database should serve to anyone who asks it. The reporter's own UI
    // remembers locally instead, which is worth a stale answer after a
    // reinstall.
    //
    // `create` is isMember() rather than canWriteEdition(): a LOCKED past year
    // must still be reportable. The lock exists to stop scores changing after
    // a cup is over, and it would be a strange rule that froze the one action
    // whose whole purpose is flagging something that should not be there.
    //
    // `reported_by` is pinned to the caller, same as bc_media's own create —
    // it is the field the director reads to know who raised it, so it has to
    // be unforgeable at the moment it is written.
    //
    // No update at all: a report is a fact about a moment, not a document to
    // revise. Reporting twice writes the same id (see reportDocId) and lands
    // as an identical create rather than an edit.
    match /bc_media_reports/{doc} {
      allow read: if isDirector();
      allow create: if isMember()
                    && request.resource.data.reported_by == request.auth.uid;
      allow update: if false;
      allow delete: if isDirector();
    }

    // ── Side bets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // One document per player-to-player wager. The app records these and
    // scores nothing — see src/lib/sideBets.js.
    //
    // Same shape as bc_media, and for the same reason: `create` pins
    // created_by to the caller's own uid, because that field is what the
    // delete rule below trusts, so it has to be unforgeable at the moment it
    // is written. `update` cannot reassign authorship — fixing a typo in the
    // terms must not be able to become a change of author.
    //
    // What these rules deliberately do NOT check is that `player_a` is the
    // caller. They cannot: finding the roster row whose auth_uid is yours
    // needs a query, and rules only fetch paths they can construct — the same
    // limit that keeps the roster crown from granting anything. So a member
    // can log a bet naming two other people. That is a feature at the bar and
    // a nuisance nowhere: the record is public to the whole field, and a bet
    // somebody did not agree to is a bet with their name on it in front of
    // everybody who would know better.
    //
    // Delete is the author's or a director's. NOT the other player's — the
    // side of a bet you dispute is not yours to erase, and the director is
    // who settles that here as everywhere else. src/lib/sideBets.js
    // canDeleteSideBet() mirrors this exactly, and sideBets.test.js pins it.
    //
    // ── The settlement mark, and why it widens update ──────────────
    // A bet is paid when BOTH players say so, which means the player who did
    // NOT log it has to be able to write to it — and author-only update made
    // that impossible. So there is a third update clause: any member may
    // change `settled_by` AND NOTHING ELSE. hasOnly() is what makes that
    // safe. It cannot touch the amount, the terms, who is in the bet, or who
    // wrote it, so the widest thing a stranger to the bet can do is add or
    // remove a name in a list the screen renders as a claim.
    //
    // The rules cannot check that the mark is the caller's own — that would
    // need the roster row whose auth_uid is yours, which needs a query, the
    // same limit that keeps the crown from granting anything. The app writes
    // only your own id. This is the trade the whole file already makes and
    // says so at the top: within membership there is no per-document
    // ownership, because scoring is communal and "only your own rows" would
    // break the workflow before it stopped anybody.
    match /bc_side_bets/{doc} {
      allow read: if isOpen();
      allow create: if canWriteEdition()
                    && request.resource.data.created_by == request.auth.uid;
      allow update: if isDirector()
                    || (canWriteEdition()
                        && resource.data.created_by == request.auth.uid
                        && request.resource.data.created_by == resource.data.created_by)
                    || (canWriteEdition()
                        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                             .hasOnly(['settled_by']));
      allow delete: if isDirector()
                    || (canWriteEdition() && resource.data.created_by == request.auth.uid);
    }

    // ── The trip ledger ───────────────────────────────────────────
    // One document per PAYMENT a player has made to the director for the
    // trip — see src/lib/ledger.js. What is owed is not here: the
    // tournament-wide figure is a bc_settings document and a per-player
    // override is a field on the roster row, both already director-only
    // above. So director-only write covers the whole feature.
    //
    // The simplest block in this file, and deliberately so. There is no
    // member-write clause because a player logging their own payment would
    // be a CLAIM, not a record — the person who received the money is the
    // one who knows it arrived. That is the same reasoning that keeps a
    // side bet unsettled until both players mark it, arrived at from the
    // other end: here the two parties are not equals, so there is no
    // handshake to build.
    //
    // Read is open, like everything else in this file, which means a member
    // could read another man's balance by talking to Firestore directly.
    // That is the same trade the roster, the handicaps and the side bets
    // already make (see the note at the top), and the screens only ever
    // show a player their own. If reads are ever tightened, this belongs in
    // the same pass as bc_notification_tokens — it is the second collection
    // here that holds something a reader has no business enumerating.
    //
    // canAdminEdition() rather than isDirector() for the demo's sake — Budget
    // is a tab a reviewer will open, both halves of it are edition-scoped, and
    // a money write is `loud` so a refused one toasts rather than vanishing.
    // That makes it the one collection here where the failure would be VISIBLE
    // and it is still the wrong thing to show somebody reviewing the app.
    match /bc_ledger/{doc}             { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }

    // The other half of Admin → $: what the trip COSTS, one document per
    // line. Same rule as the ledger above and for a simpler reason — what
    // the weekend costs is not a thing a player has an opinion about, so
    // there is no member clause to argue over.
    match /bc_budget/{doc}             { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if canAdminEdition(); }

    // ── Runtime switches ──────────────────────────────────────────
    // Today this holds exactly one document: bc_config/photos, the photo
    // library's budget circuit breaker. It is written by the onBudgetAlert
    // Cloud Function through the admin SDK, which bypasses these rules
    // entirely — so the grant below is only about the two client paths.
    //
    // Read is open because every phone has to know whether uploads are on,
    // including before anybody signs in.
    //
    // Write is DIRECTOR-only, and that is the whole point: a member must not
    // be able to clear a breaker that a budget tripped. A director can, which
    // is the manual override for "the budget was wrong" — the same person who
    // can go and raise the budget.
    match /bc_config/{doc}             { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if isDirector(); }

    // ── Push notification tokens ──────────────────────────────────
    // (Read stays open here for the same reason as everywhere else; see
    // the note below on why this is the collection to tighten first.)
    // One document per (player, device). Holds an FCM registration token,
    // which is a send-to address, not a credential: possession lets you
    // push a notification to that device, not read anything from it. The
    // Cloud Functions in functions/ read this collection through the admin
    // SDK, which bypasses these rules entirely — the grant below is only
    // for the client that registers and revokes its own token.
    //
    // Worth noting what open READ means here specifically: unlike the rest
    // of this file, where the data is already public to anyone with the
    // bundle, this collection lets a reader enumerate the league's devices.
    // That is the same trade the app makes everywhere else, but it is the
    // one collection where a future sign-in should tighten reads first.
    match /bc_notification_tokens/{doc} { allow read: if isOpen();
                                         allow write: if isMember(); }

    // ── Everything else ───────────────────────────────────────────
    // Default deny. Adding a collection to the app means adding it here.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
